description: allow a Wine prefix to be shared by several Unix users
 Wine assumes a prefix belongs to exactly one Unix user, and enforces that in
 several independent places: the prefix directory and the server directory must
 be owned by the caller and unreadable by anyone else, the server directory path
 itself contains the caller's uid, and the socket and lock file are created
 mode 0600. Those checks together are the entire access control -- the server
 authenticates nobody, because with a 0700 directory the question of who is
 connecting has a constant answer.
 .
 This adds an opt-in "system prefix" mode, marked by a .sg-system-prefix file in
 the prefix, in which:
 .
   * the server directory is derived from the prefix rather than the uid, so
     every user of it computes the same path;
   * the prefix, server directory, socket and lock file are group-accessible,
     and are given the prefix's group rather than the primary group of whoever
     happened to start the server first;
   * the ownership assertions are replaced by access checks;
   * and -- the part that makes the rest safe -- the server authenticates each
     connecting process with SO_PEERCRED, admitting root, the owner of the
     server directory, and members of the group that owns it.
 .
 The credential check is not separable from the relaxations: on its own it is
 dead code, and without it the relaxations remove a security control and put
 nothing in its place. They are one change.
 .
 Ordinary prefixes are untouched: without the marker every path behaves exactly
 as before.
 .
 Note the rule is implemented twice, in the server and in ntdll, because the
 client works out where the server lives on its own -- it may be the process
 that starts it. The two must agree exactly; patching one side alone fails with
 "chdir to /tmp/.wine-<uid>/server-<dev>-<ino>: No such file or directory",
 which names neither the cause nor the file to fix.
origin: Stained Glass OS
bug: https://github.com/Stained-Glass-OS/stained-glass/issues/2
forwarded: not-needed
 Stained Glass OS does not submit patches upstream; see ADR 0006 in the
 stained-glass repo. This changes Wine's security model for a deployment
 scenario upstream does not target, and is unlikely to be wanted there.
